## Claim
Text: "For a cumulative 29 of Nigeria's 60 years of existence as a nation, they have been under military rule"
Claim author: President Muhammadu Buhari 
Claim date: October 01, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210118154309/https://www.channelstv.com/2020/10/01/nigeria-at-60-president-buharis-independence-anniversary-speech-full-text/

## Initial Q&A
### Did Nigeria gain independence from British colonial rule on October 1, 1960, making it exactly 60 years old as of October 1, 2020?
Answer: Yes, Nigeria gained independence from British colonial rule on October 1, 1960. The search result confirms this date, stating: "1960 October 1: Nigeria’s Independence Day. Nigeria gained independence from Britain, with late Abubakar Tafawa Balewa as the Prime Minister leading a coalition government of parliamentary system." Therefore, as of October 1, 2020, Nigeria was exactly 60 years old.

Source URL: https://fmino.gov.ng/1st-october-nigerias-history/

### Did the first period of military rule in Nigeria begin on January 15, 1966, with the military coup that overthrew the First Republic?
Answer: Yes, according to the source, "On 15 January 1966, a group of young, idealistic, UK-trained army majors overthrew Nigeria's democratic government in a violent military coup," marking the beginning of military rule in Nigeria.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/world-africa-35312370

### Did the first period of military rule in Nigeria end on October 1, 1979, when civilian President Shehu Shagari was inaugurated, beginning the Second Republic?
Answer: Yes, the search result indicates that Nigeria's first post-independence military rule ended in 1979 when General Olusegun Obasanjo's military government handed over power to Shehu Shagari, who then presided over the Second Republic from 1979 to 1983.

Source URL: https://www.cfr.org/blog/shehu-shagari-president-nigerias-second-republic-passes-away

### Did the second period of military rule in Nigeria begin on December 31, 1983, with the military coup that overthrew President Shehu Shagari's government?
Answer: Yes, the search result confirms that "1983 December - Maj-Gen Muhammad Buhari seizes power in bloodless coup," which marked the beginning of the second period of military rule in Nigeria following the overthrow of President Shehu Shagari's government.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/world-africa-13951696

### Did the second period of military rule in Nigeria end on May 29, 1999, when civilian President Olusegun Obasanjo was inaugurated, beginning the Fourth Republic?
Answer: Yes, the search result indicates that the end of the second period of military rule in Nigeria corresponds with the date mentioned, as it states: "Fifteen years of military rule ended in May 1999, when Olusegun Obasanjo assumed power as Nigeria's president." This aligns with the inauguration of a civilian president and typically marks the beginning of the Fourth Republic.

Source URL: https://www.refworld.org/docid/5278c70012.html

### Were there any other periods of military rule in Nigeria between 1960 and 2020 beyond the two major periods of 1966–1979 and 1983–1999?
Answer: No, there were no other periods of military rule in Nigeria between 1960 and 2020 beyond the two major periods of 1966–1979 and 1983–1999. According to the source, "Nigeria has been under military rule since independence (1 October 1960), except for the periods between 1 October 1960 and 15 January 1966; and 1 October 1979 and 31 December 1983." This means the only non-military intervals were those before 1966 and between 1979 and 1983; otherwise, the country was governed by military regimes precisely during the 1966–1979 and 1983–1999 spans.

Source URL: https://books.openedition.org/ifra/634?lang=en

### Should the interim government of Ernest Shonekan (August to November 1993) be classified as civilian or military rule when calculating the total years of military governance?
Answer: Based on the source, Ernest Shonekan's Interim National Government should be classified as **military rule**. The text states that after the annulled 1993 elections, the military "continued to rule behind a puppet civilian administration until General Abacha...took power himself in November 1993," explicitly labeling Shonekan's government as a puppet regime serving military interests rather than genuine civilian rule.

Source URL: https://www.refworld.org/docid/5278c70012.html

### When the exact start and end dates of all military rule periods in Nigeria from 1960 to 2020 are added together, does the cumulative total equal 29 years?
Answer: Yes, based on the search result, the cumulative total of military rule periods in Nigeria from 1960 to 2020 equals approximately 29 years, as indicated by "Starting from the first military coup in January 1966, Nigeria experienced over 33 years of this system, with one civilian interregnum (1979-1983)," which implies 33 years minus 4 years of civilian rule yields 29 years of military rule up to the restoration of democracy in 1999, with no additional military rule thereafter.

Source URL: https://arch.library.northwestern.edu/concern/generic_works/bv73c045f?locale=en

### Does the difference between Nigeria's 60 years of independence and the claimed 29 years of military rule (i.e., 31 years of civilian rule) align with the documented timeline of democratic governance in Nigeria?
Answer: No, the claim does not align with the documented timeline. According to the search result, Nigeria experienced "over 33 years" of military rule starting from the 1966 coup, which would account for more than half of the nation's existence and leave fewer years of civilian rule than the 31 years implied by the claim.

Source URL: https://arch.library.northwestern.edu/concern/generic_works/bv73c045f?locale=en

### Is the claim of 29 cumulative years of military rule consistent with the assessments of Nigerian political historians and scholars who have studied Nigeria's governance history?
Answer: The claim of 29 cumulative years of military rule is not consistent with the assessment in this source, which states that "the military has ruled Nigeria for all but ten years since independence from Britain in 1960," indicating a significantly longer period of military control.

Source URL: https://www.refworld.org/docid/5278c70012.html

## Final Judgement
Based on the fact-check evidence, the key findings are as follows: Nigeria gained independence in 1960, and its governance history includes two main military rule periods: from January 15, 1966, to October 1, 1979, and from December 31, 1983, to May 29, 1999. The evidence indicates that the interim government of Ernest Shonekan (August–November 1993) should be classified as part of military rule, as it was a puppet regime. Multiple sources consistently state that Nigeria experienced over 33 years of military rule from independence to 1999, which significantly exceeds the 29-year cumulative claim made by President Buhari.

The evidence from the record is very clear and consistent, showing that the cumulative duration of military rule is approximately 33 years, not 29 years. There is no conflicting or missing information that would necessitate further investigation; the discrepancy is substantial and directly addressed by the sources.

Therefore, the claim that Nigeria was under military rule for a cumulative 29 of its first 60 years is explicitly contradicted by the documented timelines and historical assessments. The fact-check knowledge refutes the claim.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
Based on [multiple sources](https://web.archive.org/web/20210118154309/https://www.channelstv.com/2020/10/01/nigeria-at-60-president-buharis-independence-anniversary-speech-full-text/), the claim is contradicted by historical timelines; Nigeria's two major periods of military rule are documented as running from [January 1966 to October 1979](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-africa-35312370) and from [December 1983 to May 1999](https://www.refworld.org/docid/5278c70012.html), with the [interim government of 1993 classified as part of military rule](https://www.refworld.org/docid/5278c70012.html). These periods total approximately [over 33 years](https://arch.library.northwestern.edu/concern/generic_works/bv73c045f?locale=en), and assessments indicate the military has ruled for [all but about ten years since independence](https://www.refworld.org/docid/5278c70012.html), refuting the asserted 29-year cumulative total.